1
00:00:13,667 --> 00:00:20,851
Here is the sentence the whole of Part Two
turns on. It is on page six hundred and twelve,

2
00:00:20,851 --> 00:00:23,965
and these are the authors' exact words.

3
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:33,259
It contains two completely different kinds of
claim, stitched together. Split it in half.

4
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:41,419
The first half is a question about prices.
Does the cheaper money actually arrive at a firm?

5
00:00:41,419 --> 00:00:43,402
You can go and check that.

6
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:50,230
The second half is a question about behaviour.
What does a firm do once the money has

7
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:54,370
arrived? That is a different sort of question
entirely.

8
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,898
The first half gets hedged. Banks were
impaired.

9
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:05,842
Households and smaller companies have no
access to capital markets.

10
00:01:06,267 --> 00:01:09,090
So they may not directly benefit.

11
00:01:09,533 --> 00:01:16,137
And be fair to them. These are honest, careful
qualifications. They also name several other

12
00:01:16,137 --> 00:01:22,668
routes by which small firms might still
benefit — through supply chains, through a cheaper

13
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:29,271
currency helping exporters, and through banks
passing on lower rates. All of that is in the

14
00:01:29,271 --> 00:01:29,851
article.

15
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:33,893
Now Kit waits for the same thing to happen to
the other half.

16
00:01:37,733 --> 00:01:44,831
One half of that sentence is hedged three
times. The other half is not hedged once.

17
00:01:48,367 --> 00:01:53,962
His first reaction is not disagreement, and
that matters for everything that follows.

18
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:59,155
His first reaction is that two halves of one
sentence are not the same kind of statement,

19
00:01:59,155 --> 00:02:01,559
and are being given the same amount of trust.

20
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:12,686
This is not carelessness. It is what a certain
kind of economics does by construction. It

21
00:02:12,686 --> 00:02:20,357
models markets in enormous detail, and it
treats a firm as a machine. Numbers in. Decision

22
00:02:20,357 --> 00:02:20,698
out.

23
00:02:21,133 --> 00:02:28,202
There is nothing dishonest in that. It is a
simplification, and simplifications are how

24
00:02:28,202 --> 00:02:31,533
anyone thinks about anything complicated.

25
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:39,482
But it does mean that the last link in the
chain is the only one nobody examines.

26
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:45,975
one sentence, two different kinds of claim

27
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:49,461
the price half is hedged three times

28
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,764
the behaviour half is not hedged once

29
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,099
because a firm is modelled as a machine

30
00:03:04,499 --> 00:03:10,772
A page later, the authors ask a question about
somebody else. What does the government do

31
00:03:10,772 --> 00:03:13,309
when its own borrowing gets cheaper?

32
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:20,157
And notice what they open with. The
government's borrowing cost is now lower. Gilts are

33
00:03:20,157 --> 00:03:26,730
exactly what the Bank had been buying. So if
anyone got the cheaper price, the government

34
00:03:26,730 --> 00:03:27,025
did.

35
00:03:27,466 --> 00:03:30,061
The money definitely arrives.

36
00:03:31,899 --> 00:03:33,905
And nothing happens.

37
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:45,925
Here is what the authors say, in their own
words. Their spending plans should therefore be

38
00:03:45,925 --> 00:03:49,830
unaffected by cyclical movements in interest
rates.

39
00:03:52,066 --> 00:03:57,952
And look at the reason. It has nothing to do
with access. The money arrives.

40
00:03:58,399 --> 00:04:05,311
The reason is about the horizon the decision
is taken over. Governments plan a long way

41
00:04:05,311 --> 00:04:05,788
ahead.

42
00:04:09,933 --> 00:04:15,281
Kit gets excited. They have admitted, he says,
that a feature of how the decision is made

43
00:04:15,281 --> 00:04:16,843
can break the whole chain.

44
00:04:18,666 --> 00:04:23,788
And then he reads it again, and crosses his
own conclusion out. That is the first of several

45
00:04:23,788 --> 00:04:25,013
times he will do that.

46
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:34,209
What survives is this, and it is thinner than
he first thought. A feature of the decision

47
00:04:34,209 --> 00:04:36,063
itself is allowed, once,

48
00:04:36,499 --> 00:04:40,765
to break the link between a cheaper price and
more spending.

49
00:04:41,199 --> 00:04:47,985
And nobody then asks whether anything else
about how a decision gets made could do the same.

50
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:56,551
That is a reason to go and look. It is not
evidence about what you find.

51
00:04:59,199 --> 00:05:05,866
That sentence is the intellectual standard the
rest of the film is held to. Everything Kit

52
00:05:05,866 --> 00:05:08,089
says from here has to meet it.

53
00:05:10,933 --> 00:05:14,503
the government gets the cheaper money for
certain

54
00:05:14,633 --> 00:05:18,180
and its plans are unaffected — their words

55
00:05:18,299 --> 00:05:21,644
the reason is the horizon, not access

56
00:05:21,766 --> 00:05:25,266
which is a reason to look, not a finding

57
00:05:33,666 --> 00:05:41,353
Kit puts the two articles side by side, and
notices they are arguing about the same quantity

58
00:05:41,353 --> 00:05:43,024
from opposite sides.

59
00:05:43,466 --> 00:05:48,659
One of them asserts that a lower cost of
capital raises investment.

60
00:05:49,099 --> 00:05:55,083
And the other spends a whole article showing
that the number standing between the cost of

61
00:05:55,083 --> 00:05:58,579
capital and the decision is not the cost of
capital.

62
00:06:02,099 --> 00:06:07,074
The bar a project must clear is the textbook
bar, multiplied.

63
00:06:07,499 --> 00:06:12,218
And the multiplier grows as the world becomes
more uncertain.

64
00:06:15,066 --> 00:06:21,420
Which means we can go back to the sheet from
chapter eight, and watch what the policy

65
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:23,663
actually does to a firm's bar.

66
00:06:30,499 --> 00:06:38,103
Here is a firm in ordinary times. Twenty per
cent choppiness, money at five per cent. Its

67
00:06:38,103 --> 00:06:41,862
bar is one point eight six times break-even.

68
00:06:43,699 --> 00:06:50,931
The policy makes money cheaper. So the firm
slides this way — and the sheet under it goes

69
00:06:50,931 --> 00:06:53,531
up, not down. Two point six one.

70
00:06:55,366 --> 00:07:01,968
But the policy is only ever used in a crisis.
So at the very same moment, the firm is

71
00:07:01,968 --> 00:07:07,017
dragged the other way too — into the choppiest
part of the sheet.

72
00:07:08,866 --> 00:07:14,568
Both movements push the bar up. Not one up and
one down. Both up.

73
00:07:17,399 --> 00:07:24,305
Which gives the sentence this chapter exists
for. Quantitative easing is not a weaker

74
00:07:24,305 --> 00:07:31,372
version of a rate cut. It is a rate cut
delivered in the one situation that blunts rate

75
00:07:31,372 --> 00:07:31,778
cuts.

76
00:07:36,166 --> 00:07:43,931
And be clear about whose is whose. The bar,
and the fact that it rises with uncertainty, is

77
00:07:43,931 --> 00:07:44,613
Dixit's.

78
00:07:45,033 --> 00:07:48,085
Putting it next to this policy is Kit's.

79
00:07:50,933 --> 00:07:55,153
one article asserts what the other spends
pages on

80
00:07:55,299 --> 00:07:58,312
cheaper money moves the firm up the sheet

81
00:07:58,433 --> 00:08:02,107
and a crisis moves it up the other way too

82
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:06,221
a rate cut delivered where rate cuts are
blunted

83
00:08:14,632 --> 00:08:18,206
Cheap money is meant to make building
attractive.

84
00:08:18,632 --> 00:08:21,916
But cheap money also makes waiting cheaper.

85
00:08:22,365 --> 00:08:28,342
Because the cost of holding off for a year is
the return the money would have earned

86
00:08:28,342 --> 00:08:32,753
meanwhile. Make that return small, and holding
off hurts less.

87
00:08:36,799 --> 00:08:42,759
At a five per cent cost of capital, the
multiplier is one point eight six.

88
00:08:43,199 --> 00:08:46,769
At two per cent, it is two point six one.

89
00:08:50,299 --> 00:08:57,123
Think of a firm's bar as two pieces. The
break-even level, and the mark-up that waiting adds

90
00:08:57,123 --> 00:08:58,088
on top of it.

91
00:08:58,532 --> 00:09:01,440
The policy pushes the bottom piece down.

92
00:09:01,865 --> 00:09:05,932
And at the same time it fattens the piece on
top.

93
00:09:07,765 --> 00:09:14,284
The direction is not in doubt. The bar does
come down. The first effect wins.

94
00:09:17,832 --> 00:09:22,601
What Kit originally wanted to say was sharper.
That it comes down by less than you would

95
00:09:22,601 --> 00:09:22,980
expect.

96
00:09:23,399 --> 00:09:28,293
He has withdrawn it. Because whether that is
true depends entirely on how you measure it.

97
00:09:28,732 --> 00:09:35,337
Compare the two figures as percentage points,
and the bar falls by more than the cost of

98
00:09:35,337 --> 00:09:36,162
money does.

99
00:09:36,599 --> 00:09:41,050
Compare them as proportions, and it falls by
less.

100
00:09:41,499 --> 00:09:46,160
Both sums are correct. Neither of them is the
true one. So he declines to pick.

101
00:09:48,999 --> 00:09:55,643
The claim he is entitled to is the plain one.
Part of the policy is spent making patience

102
00:09:55,643 --> 00:09:56,837
more attractive.

103
00:10:01,465 --> 00:10:05,523
cheap money makes building and waiting both
cheaper

104
00:10:05,665 --> 00:10:13,020
1.86 at 5% · 2.61 at 2% — Dixit's own figures

105
00:10:13,165 --> 00:10:16,794
the bar does fall: the first effect wins

106
00:10:16,932 --> 00:10:20,525
but part of the policy is spent on patience

107
00:10:28,932 --> 00:10:35,895
Replay the bad news principle. When you can
wait, it is mainly the bad possible outcomes

108
00:10:35,895 --> 00:10:38,586
that decide whether you build now.

109
00:10:40,432 --> 00:10:45,346
So the question to ask is: what does this
policy actually compress?

110
00:10:48,865 --> 00:10:55,838
In financial markets, there is a price for
carrying risk. Quantitative easing pushes that

111
00:10:55,838 --> 00:10:58,266
price down. Squeeze the spring.

112
00:11:01,799 --> 00:11:08,055
But the doubt inside a firm is a different
thing. Will anyone still be buying in three

113
00:11:08,055 --> 00:11:11,110
years? Will this factory still make sense?

114
00:11:11,532 --> 00:11:18,443
Those are different worries entirely. A
central bank buying government bonds reaches them

115
00:11:18,443 --> 00:11:20,539
only indirectly, if at all.

116
00:11:22,365 --> 00:11:27,330
So the honest version of his claim is about
how directly, not about whether.

117
00:11:30,165 --> 00:11:36,190
What a central bank reaches directly is the
price at which risk trades.

118
00:11:36,632 --> 00:11:41,421
What it reaches at one or two removes is the
doubt itself.

119
00:11:44,265 --> 00:11:48,219
the bad half decides whether she builds now

120
00:11:48,365 --> 00:11:52,028
the policy squeezes the price of risk directly

121
00:11:52,165 --> 00:11:55,805
the doubt inside a firm, only indirectly

122
00:11:55,932 --> 00:11:59,675
so the claim is about how directly, not
whether

123
00:12:06,599 --> 00:12:11,737
This chapter exists because Kit's own source
damages him, and that is what intellectual

124
00:12:11,737 --> 00:12:12,859
honesty looks like.

125
00:12:14,899 --> 00:12:22,452
Replay the two countries. Japanese firms
invested boldly and hung on through losses, because

126
00:12:22,452 --> 00:12:24,833
their downside was cushioned.

127
00:12:31,065 --> 00:12:36,736
Which half of that spread does a policy that
props up prices and signals that the

128
00:12:36,736 --> 00:12:39,396
authorities will act actually work on?

129
00:12:39,832 --> 00:12:46,444
This half. Which is exactly the right half for
keeping a struggling firm from closing.

130
00:12:49,965 --> 00:12:56,763
A policy that props up the upside is aimed at
exactly the right half of the distribution for

131
00:12:56,763 --> 00:13:03,412
keeping firms alive. Which hands the policy a
mechanism for the very thing he was going to

132
00:13:03,412 --> 00:13:04,890
explain another way.

133
00:13:06,732 --> 00:13:11,310
Why include something that damages you?
Because it is what his own source says. The

134
00:13:11,310 --> 00:13:12,633
discomfort is the point.

135
00:13:15,465 --> 00:13:19,151
a cushioned downside keeps firms invested

136
00:13:19,299 --> 00:13:22,184
the policy props up the good half

137
00:13:22,332 --> 00:13:26,123
which is the right half for keeping firms
alive

138
00:13:26,265 --> 00:13:29,894
so his own source hands the policy a mechanism

139
00:13:38,298 --> 00:13:45,357
A third article, and a third pair of authors.
Christopher Martin and Costas Milas. Their job

140
00:13:45,357 --> 00:13:52,186
was to gather up the studies of what the
policy actually achieved. Their colour is green.

141
00:13:54,332 --> 00:14:01,318
Across the studies they gathered, quantitative
easing raised growth by somewhere between one

142
00:14:01,318 --> 00:14:05,495
and three per cent, with a similar effect on
inflation.

143
00:14:09,032 --> 00:14:16,001
And here is their verdict, in their own words.
Quantitative easing has proved effective in

144
00:14:16,001 --> 00:14:23,047
limiting the scale of the downturn. However,
by itself, it is not strong enough to spark an

145
00:14:23,047 --> 00:14:24,441
economic recovery.

146
00:14:26,465 --> 00:14:33,644
And they hedge it themselves. This conclusion,
they say, is tentative. There is relatively

147
00:14:33,644 --> 00:14:37,872
little evidence, and most of it uses the same
method.

148
00:14:41,398 --> 00:14:49,006
In one line: effective at preventing a deeper
crisis, less effective at promoting recovery.

149
00:14:50,432 --> 00:14:56,204
The first time Kit read that, he treated it as
a result. Here is what the numbers say. Write

150
00:14:56,204 --> 00:14:57,270
it down, move on.

151
00:14:57,698 --> 00:15:01,477
Reading it again, after Dixit, he cannot let
it alone.

152
00:15:01,898 --> 00:15:07,185
Why would one policy be good at stopping a
fall and bad at starting a rise? Those are not

153
00:15:07,185 --> 00:15:08,670
obviously different jobs.

154
00:15:09,098 --> 00:15:14,060
And he is uncomfortable. Not with the article
— with himself, for having read that sentence

155
00:15:14,060 --> 00:15:17,659
once already and never asked whether there was
a reason behind it.

156
00:15:21,198 --> 00:15:27,628
Preventing a collapse needs markets that keep
working and the upside held up. And by the

157
00:15:27,628 --> 00:15:34,205
good news principle, that is exactly what
stops a struggling firm closing. It is strongest

158
00:15:34,205 --> 00:15:40,635
when things are frightening, because there is
most to repair and most upside to restore.

159
00:15:41,065 --> 00:15:47,470
Starting a recovery needs firms to commit
money they cannot get back. By the bad news

160
00:15:47,470 --> 00:15:54,326
principle, that turns on the downside — which
is exactly what fear makes them least willing

161
00:15:54,326 --> 00:15:57,792
to face. It is strongest when things are calm.

162
00:16:00,632 --> 00:16:08,161
So the policy is used in exactly the state
where the first job is easiest and the second is

163
00:16:08,161 --> 00:16:08,823
hardest.

164
00:16:09,265 --> 00:16:11,582
It steadies without starting.

165
00:16:13,798 --> 00:16:20,495
And flag clearly which parts of that are Kit's
and not Dixit's. That propping up the upside

166
00:16:20,495 --> 00:16:26,897
works best when things are frightening, and
that the two jobs therefore pull apart, are

167
00:16:26,897 --> 00:16:31,312
steps Kit has added. Dixit's own text does not
contain them.

168
00:16:34,132 --> 00:16:38,596
their verdict: prevents a crisis, less so a
recovery

169
00:16:38,732 --> 00:16:42,348
the two jobs need opposite halves of the
spread

170
00:16:42,465 --> 00:16:45,652
and opposite conditions to work in

171
00:16:45,798 --> 00:16:50,321
it steadies without starting — Kit's step, not
Dixit's

172
00:16:59,732 --> 00:17:07,166
You will recognise this picture instantly,
because it is the hysteresis one. Same scale,

173
00:17:07,166 --> 00:17:10,883
same lines. Only the direction is different.

174
00:17:11,332 --> 00:17:13,857
A firm that is already running.

175
00:17:14,298 --> 00:17:18,136
The crisis arrives, and its takings fall.

176
00:17:18,565 --> 00:17:25,291
Down past the level where the textbook would
have closed it. And still it holds on, because

177
00:17:25,291 --> 00:17:28,691
holding on keeps the chance of recovery alive.

178
00:17:29,132 --> 00:17:32,723
And then through the quit line. It closes.

179
00:17:34,565 --> 00:17:41,293
Now the policy works. Conditions come back up
to normal. All the way back to where they

180
00:17:41,293 --> 00:17:41,912
started.

181
00:17:43,732 --> 00:17:50,412
The firm does not come back. Because coming
back means paying the whole set-up cost over

182
00:17:50,412 --> 00:17:50,868
again.

183
00:17:53,698 --> 00:17:59,741
State it plainly. What the policy stops from
happening does not need explaining.

184
00:18:00,165 --> 00:18:07,183
What needs explaining is why, for the firms it
did not reach in time, nothing came back.

185
00:18:11,632 --> 00:18:15,213
a firm absorbs losses, then closes

186
00:18:15,332 --> 00:18:17,961
conditions recover all the way

187
00:18:18,098 --> 00:18:20,740
and the firm does not come back

188
00:18:20,865 --> 00:18:24,284
because coming back costs the set-up again

189
00:18:31,231 --> 00:18:36,558
Four doors. Kit opens each one, and cannot
close it again. This is the chapter where the

190
00:18:36,558 --> 00:18:40,372
film earns its credibility, so none of these
gets knocked down.

191
00:18:42,798 --> 00:18:49,352
Door one. Firms may not have been short of
confidence at all. They may simply have been

192
00:18:49,352 --> 00:18:55,830
unable to borrow. If the banks would not lend,
no bar inside the firm was ever tested.

193
00:18:56,264 --> 00:19:02,780
Door two. They may have had no customers. If
nobody is buying, patience has nothing to do

194
00:19:02,780 --> 00:19:09,222
with it. And which of the two does more of the
work is a question this argument does not

195
00:19:09,222 --> 00:19:09,734
settle.

196
00:19:10,164 --> 00:19:17,077
Door three. If several firms are racing for
the same opportunity, waiting is not possible,

197
00:19:17,077 --> 00:19:19,841
and the textbook is right after all.

198
00:19:20,264 --> 00:19:27,288
Door four. The policy did move prices in
financial markets. That is the best-established

199
00:19:27,288 --> 00:19:32,636
finding in the whole body of research, and
nothing here touches it.

200
00:19:34,464 --> 00:19:38,683
All four are left standing open. Kit does not
claim to have closed any of them.

201
00:19:41,531 --> 00:19:48,781
Which fixes the scope of the whole argument.
It applies to investment a company can take its

202
00:19:48,781 --> 00:19:50,751
time over. Not to a race.

203
00:19:55,198 --> 00:19:58,385
firms may have been unable to borrow

204
00:19:58,531 --> 00:20:01,288
or simply have had no customers

205
00:20:01,431 --> 00:20:04,363
a race removes the option to wait

206
00:20:04,498 --> 00:20:07,766
and the policy did move market prices

207
00:20:16,164 --> 00:20:22,242
Grow Nell's factory into a large company, with
several separately run parts.

208
00:20:24,064 --> 00:20:29,547
So say this explicitly, because it is easy to
miss. Kit's dispute is not about how high the

209
00:20:29,547 --> 00:20:32,499
number is. It is about what kind of number it
is.

210
00:20:36,031 --> 00:20:41,756
And here is the mechanism, as an actual queue
of projects at the boardroom door.

211
00:20:42,198 --> 00:20:48,699
State the assumption openly, because it is
doing a lot of work and it might be wrong. In a

212
00:20:48,699 --> 00:20:54,117
large company the thing that runs out first is
not money. It is management.

213
00:20:55,964 --> 00:21:01,985
A minimum return set to solve that problem is
tied to the next project in the queue — the

214
00:21:01,985 --> 00:21:06,787
best thing the company would have to drop in
order to take this one on.

215
00:21:07,231 --> 00:21:13,680
And that is an internal quantity. It depends
on how many good ideas the business is

216
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:17,798
generating, and how many capable people it has
spare.

217
00:21:21,331 --> 00:21:27,339
A fall in the return on government debt does
not lengthen anybody's working week.

218
00:21:27,764 --> 00:21:31,528
The yield falls. And the queue does not move.

219
00:21:35,064 --> 00:21:40,001
So here is the version he can defend. Not that
the number never moves. But that it moves on

220
00:21:40,001 --> 00:21:42,768
the company's clock rather than the central
bank's.

221
00:21:43,198 --> 00:21:48,482
Changing as the pipeline of ideas and the
stock of managers change, over years. For a policy

222
00:21:48,482 --> 00:21:53,593
meant to work inside a downturn, that is close
enough to failure to matter. But it is not

223
00:21:53,593 --> 00:21:56,178
the same as never, and he will not say never.

224
00:21:59,698 --> 00:22:06,841
Treating a hurdle rate as a rationing device
rather than a calculation is not an idea Kit

225
00:22:06,841 --> 00:22:07,564
invented.

226
00:22:07,998 --> 00:22:11,254
What he would claim is the use he has put it
to.

227
00:22:14,098 --> 00:22:18,167
a big company rations management, not money

228
00:22:18,298 --> 00:22:22,344
so the bar is tied to the next project in the
queue

229
00:22:22,464 --> 00:22:26,151
the yield falls, and the queue does not move

230
00:22:26,298 --> 00:22:30,251
it moves on the company's clock, not the
Bank's

231
00:22:38,664 --> 00:22:43,438
This is not a claim that quantitative easing
does not work.

232
00:22:43,864 --> 00:22:49,729
Kit has not disputed that it moved prices in
financial markets, which is the

233
00:22:49,729 --> 00:22:53,974
best-established finding in the whole body of
research.

234
00:22:54,398 --> 00:22:57,701
It is not a claim that the theory is false.

235
00:22:58,131 --> 00:23:03,542
It is a claim that the theory is incomplete,
in one specific place.

236
00:23:08,264 --> 00:23:11,486
So rebuild the chain one final time.

237
00:23:11,931 --> 00:23:16,801
Careful reasoning about what can go wrong at
every stage.

238
00:23:17,231 --> 00:23:24,638
Except one. The stage where a quantity finally
has to change, because somebody in a room has

239
00:23:24,638 --> 00:23:25,523
to say yes.

240
00:23:29,764 --> 00:23:36,786
The first is Dixit's, and Kit is mostly
reporting it. When spending cannot be undone and the

241
00:23:36,786 --> 00:23:43,350
future is unknown, the bar is higher than the
textbook says. And it rises as the world

242
00:23:43,350 --> 00:23:45,258
becomes more frightening.

243
00:23:45,698 --> 00:23:52,582
So a policy used in frightening times is
working against the largest version of that bar.

244
00:23:52,582 --> 00:23:59,390
That is well-established economics, published
in a general economics journal, and simply

245
00:23:59,390 --> 00:24:02,329
absent from the article he began with.

246
00:24:02,764 --> 00:24:10,063
The second is Kit's. And he holds it loosely,
for all the reasons the four open doors gave.

247
00:24:12,898 --> 00:24:16,770
The gap is not a mistake in anybody's
reasoning.

248
00:24:17,198 --> 00:24:18,945
It is a boundary.

249
00:24:22,364 --> 00:24:29,053
A model of the whole economy hands off to a
decision it does not model. And the study of how

250
00:24:29,053 --> 00:24:35,597
that decision actually gets made sits in a
different journal, on a different reading list,

251
00:24:35,597 --> 00:24:38,578
and in the other half of the same degree.

252
00:24:42,698 --> 00:24:49,215
And the last shot is all of them, standing
either side of the chain, at the link nobody

253
00:24:49,215 --> 00:24:49,889
examined.

254
00:24:53,331 --> 00:24:57,621
not that the policy fails, or the theory is
false

255
00:24:57,764 --> 00:25:01,311
that the theory is incomplete in one place

256
00:25:01,431 --> 00:25:05,547
the bar rises exactly when the policy is used

257
00:25:05,664 --> 00:25:09,618
and the last link is a boundary, not a mistake
